 it was using `false_discovery_control`. Is that in statsmodels.stats.multitest? Hmm, does statsmodels have `false_discovery_control`? Not sure. Anyway, BH computed manually is well-defined; but the exact tie-handling/ordering may differ slightly. Implement standard BH: sort ascending, adj[i] = p[i]*m/rank, then enforce monotonicity from the largest downward via cummin, and cap at 1. My earlier code had a bug: `bh[order] = p.values[order] * m / np.arange(1, m+1)` — wait, this assigns p[order]*m/(1..m) to positions order… let me redo: sorted_p = p[order]; adj_sorted = sorted_p * m / rank; adj_sorted = np.minimum.accumulate(adj_sorted[::-1])[::-1]; then bh[order] = adj_sorted. My code assigned `bh[order] = p.values[order] * m / arange(...)` — p.values[order] is exactly sorted_p, so that's fine. Then `bh = np.minimum.accumulate(bh[::-1])[::-1]` — wrong! cummin needs to be applied in sorted order, not in original index order. That's a bug in my verification. Fix it.\n\nSo the earlier max diff of ~1.0 was probably due to my own bug. Fix BH, redo with the family = padj non-null set (which should be the genes passing the cutoff), and additionally verify the separation property between baseMean of the null set and non-null set.\n\nAlso verify n_padj_null: nulls = excluded by cutoff + genes with null p-values. pvalue nulls occur for genes excluded by Cooks filtering or by zero-count conditions. Let me count pvalue nulls in the CSV.\n"}}
